        <h1 class="profile-detail-name">Rani of Jhansi</h1>
        <div class="info-stats" style="margin-top:10px;">
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Period</span><span class="info-stat-val">c. 1828 CE – 1858 CE</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Born</span><span class="info-stat-val"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/varanasi">Varanasi</a>, as Manikarnika Tambe, called Manu; daughter of Moropant Tambe, a Karhada <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brahmins">Brahmin</a> in the service of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maratha-empire">Maratha</a> noble Chimaji, and Bhagirathi. Her birth year is disputed: 1827 in British sources, 1835 in most Indian sources, between 1828 and 1830 for the historians Tapti Roy and Rudrangshu Mukherjee</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Died</span><span class="info-stat-val">18 June 1858, in battle against British forces near Gwalior; the exact manner of her death is unknown, but all accounts agree she died fighting</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Who they were</span><span class="info-stat-val">Queen consort of the princely state of Jhansi from 1843 to 1853; leader of Jhansi from June 1857 until the British captured it in April 1858; one of the leading figures of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indian-rebellion-of-1857">Indian Rebellion of 1857</a></span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Known for</span><span class="info-stat-val">her appeals against the annexation of Jhansi under the doctrine of lapse; her rule of Jhansi in 1857 to 1858; the Siege of Jhansi and her escape from the fort; the last stand at Gwalior; the contested question of her part in the killing of the British at Jhansi; her place in Indian nationalism</span></div>
