    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>Married as a child to the raja of Jhansi, Lakshmibai lost her infant son and then her husband, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-india-company">East India Company</a> annexed Jhansi in 1854 under the doctrine of lapse in spite of her appeals.</li><li>After sepoys mutinied at Jhansi in June 1857 and nearly all the British there were killed, she took over its rule; whether she took part in the killings was and remains contested.</li><li>Hugh Rose captured Jhansi in April 1858, and she died fighting at Gwalior that June; she became a heroine of Indian nationalism, a reading that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dalit">Dalit</a> writers dispute.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>In 1943 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/subhas-chandra-bose">Subhas Chandra Bose</a>, recognising the Rani's symbolism, called for female volunteers for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indian-national-army">Indian National Army</a>, who were formed into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rani-of-jhansi-regiment">Rani of Jhansi Regiment</a> and trained in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/singapore">Singapore</a>.</p></aside>
    </section>

