    <section class="rise-fall" id="timeline" aria-labelledby="timeline-title">
      <h2 class="profile-section-title" id="timeline-title">Timeline</h2>
      <ol class="rf-events"><li><span class="rf-year">c. 1828</span><span class="rf-text">Manikarnika Tambe is born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/varanasi">Varanasi</a>, a date placed between 1828 and 1830 by Roy and Mukherjee but given as 1827 or 1835 in other sources</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1842</span><span class="rf-text">According to Indian sources, she marries the raja of Jhansi, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gangadhar-rao">Gangadhar Rao</a>, in May and becomes the Rani Lakshmibai</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1851</span><span class="rf-text">She gives birth to a son, who dies at a few months old</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1853</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gangadhar-rao">Gangadhar Rao</a> adopts Damodar Rao and dies on 21 November</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1854</span><span class="rf-text">The Company rejects her appeals, and Jhansi lapses to it in May</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1857</span><span class="rf-text">Sepoys mutiny at Jhansi in June, most of the British are killed on 8 June, and she proclaims her rule on the sepoys' departure</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1858</span><span class="rf-text">Hugh Rose besieges Jhansi from 22 March; the town falls on 3 April and she escapes from the fort that night</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1858</span><span class="rf-text">She dies in battle near Gwalior on 18 June, and Rose captures the city the next day</span></li></ol>
    </section>


    <div class="profile-section-block" id="early">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Early Life</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Little is known for certain about Lakshmibai's life before 1857, and every biography of her relies on a mixture of factual evidence and legendary tales, especially for her childhood and adolescence. She was born Manikarnika Tambe in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/varanasi">Varanasi</a>, the daughter of Moropant Tambe, a Karhada <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brahmins">Brahmin</a> who served the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maratha-empire">Maratha</a> noble Chimaji, and his wife Bhagirathi. Manikarnika was an epithet of the River <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ganges-plain">Ganges</a>, and in childhood she was known as Manu. Her birth year is disputed. British sources tended towards 1827 and Indian sources generally preferred 1835, but the historians Tapti Roy and Rudrangshu Mukherjee have argued that the later date is implausible because of chronological irregularities, and place her birth between 1828 and 1830.</p><p>Her mother died when she was four, a year after the death of Chimaji. Moropant then moved to the court of Baji Rao at Bithur, who had been deposed as Maratha peshwa in 1817; Baji Rao gave him a job and became fond of the girl. According to uncorroborated popular legend, her playmates at Bithur included Nana Sahib and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tatya-tope">Tatya Tope</a>, who would also become prominent in 1857. The stories say that she was allowed to play and learn with them, that she was literate and skilled in horseriding, and that she was given lessons in fencing, swordplay and even firearms, which would have been extremely unusual for a girl.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="rise">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Rise</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/gangadhar-rao"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/gangadhar-rao.jpg" alt="Gangadhar Rao" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/gangadhar-rao">Gangadhar Rao</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Gangadhar_Rao_alias_Babasaheb_Newalkar.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Unknown author, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>It is presumed that Baji Rao brought Manikarnika to the attention of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gangadhar-rao">Gangadhar Rao</a>, the old raja of Jhansi, who had no children and greatly desired an heir. Moropant accepted the marriage offer, and according to Indian sources the couple wed in May 1842. On that chronology she was seven years old and the marriage was not consummated until she was fourteen. She was given the name <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lakshmi">Lakshmi</a>, after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a> goddess, and was thereafter the Rani Lakshmibai. Both British and Indian sources portray Gangadhar Rao as uninterested in rulership, but British sources call him debauched and imbecilic while Indian sources read the same traits as signs of a cultured nature. According to popular legend he turned a blind eye to her equipping and training of an armed all-female regiment, though if it existed it was probably formed after his death.</p><p>In 1851 she gave birth to a son amid much rejoicing, but he died at a few months old. As the raja's health failed, he adopted on his deathbed a five-year-old relative, Anand Rao, renamed Damodar Rao, and died on 21 November 1853. Two days before, he had written to Company officials asking them to recognise Damodar Rao as ruler and Lakshmibai as his regent. Jhansi had been ceded to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-india-company">East India Company</a> in 1817, and the Company had for several years enforced a doctrine of lapse, annexing states whose Hindu rulers died without a natural heir.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="deeds">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">What They Did</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/lord-dalhousie"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/lord-dalhousie.jpg" alt="Lord Dalhousie" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/lord-dalhousie">Lord Dalhousie</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Dalhousie.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">George Richmond, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Lakshmibai wrote on 16 February 1854 to protest against the annexation, and Governor-General <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lord-dalhousie">Lord Dalhousie</a> answered eleven days later. He described <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gangadhar-rao">Gangadhar Rao</a>'s rule as one of decline and mismanagement, said that Jhansi would benefit from direct British rule, and argued that since the British had conquered the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maratha-empire">Marathas</a>, Jhansi's former overlords, the Company was the paramount power with authority over the succession. She pursued the case through diplomatic channels, talking with a sympathetic Company official, Major Ellis, and engaging the Australian lawyer John Lang. Her appeals cited treaties of 1803, 1817 and 1842 that recognised the rajas of Jhansi, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a> shastra tradition on adoption. Dalhousie rejected them without refuting their arguments, and Jhansi lapsed to the Company in May 1854. She received a lifetime pension of five thousand <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rupee">rupees</a> per month (or £6,000 yearly) and had to leave the fort but kept the palace. The Company held her liable for 36,000 rupees of her husband's debt, an issue never resolved.</p><p>After the sepoys left Jhansi on 11 June 1857, she officially proclaimed that she had assumed its rulership. Major Erskine, commissioner of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sagar-madhya-pradesh">Saugor</a> division, authorised her to rule until the British returned. She collected taxes, repaired the fort and distributed donations to the poor. Her general Jawahar Singh defeated Sadasheo Rao, who twice tried to claim Jhansi, and Jhansi repelled Orchha and Datia, which invaded on 10 August and besieged it until late October, when the raja of Banpur's troops drove them off. She then ordered the recruitment of troops and the manufacture of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cannon">cannon</a>.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="opponents">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Opponents &amp; Critics</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>On 5 June 1857 sepoys at Jhansi took the ammunition store and shot their British commanding officer, and the remaining sixty British men, women and children took refuge in the fort. On 8 June they surrendered and asked for safe passage; they were led to the Jokhan Bagh garden, where nearly all of them were killed. The Rani's part in the killings is debated. S. Thornton, a tax collector, wrote that she had instigated the revolt, and two somewhat questionable eyewitness accounts said that she executed three British messengers and falsely promised the rest safe passage. Other contemporary reports held her innocent, and the official report by F.W. Pinkney came to no clear conclusion. In two mid-June letters to Erskine she said that she had been at the mercy of the mutineers. Erskine believed her, but his superiors were less trusting; items belonging to the dead were later found in her palace, and Roy has pointed to the active part of her father Moropant. She had supplied the sepoys with a thousand men and two guns after they threatened her palace and her life.</p><p>The British probably decided she was an enemy because of her conflict with Orchha and the rebels' presence in Jhansi, and religious anti-British pamphlets wrongly attributed to her reached British intelligence. Charles Canning later declared her a rebel leader and set a bounty of 20,000 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rupee">rupees</a> on her. Indian narratives have struggled most with the massacre: either she took part from the outset and so stained her virtue, or she was a reluctant participant, which makes her later heroism less intentional than the nationalist account demands.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="last">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Last Years</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/gwalior-fort"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/gwalior-fort.jpg" alt="Gwalior Fort" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/gwalior-fort">Gwalior Fort</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Gwalior_Fort_front.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Anuppyr007, CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Major General Hugh Rose, commanding the Central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> Field Force, reached Jhansi in March 1858. By then the Rani was still sending conciliations to the British while preparing arms, and some advisers urged peace, others, including her father, war. Her troops may have decided the matter by demanding to fight. The siege began on 22 March with artillery fire, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tatya-tope">Tatya Tope</a>'s relief army of more than 20,000 was defeated at the Battle of Betwa on 1 April. British columns entered the town at 3 am on 3 April. The Rani led a counter-attack with 1,500 Afghan troops but was driven back, and the British killed around three thousand people. Her father Moropant escaped, was captured and was executed on 20 April. She left the fort that night with about three hundred soldiers, her adopted son tied to her waist; accounts of how she escaped differ and the British one told by Lyster is questionable.</p><p>She reached Kalpi at midnight on 5 April. The rebel army there passed her over for command in favour of Tatya Tope, and after Rose's victories at Kunch and Kalpi, where her advice was ignored, the rebels moved to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gwalior-fort">Gwalior Fort</a>. On 17 June Rose attacked. At about 7:30 am the Eighth Hussars scattered her bodyguard, and an eyewitness said she charged them. The manner of her death is unknown. She died on 18 June 1858, probably cremated by her followers, and Rose took Gwalior the next day. Damodar Rao lost the 600,000 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rupee">rupees</a> held for him and received a pension of 150 rupees a month.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="legacy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Legacy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/shivaji"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/shivaji.jpg" alt="Shivaji" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/shivaji">Shivaji</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Shivaji_British_Museum.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Unknown author, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Rose wrote that she was "remarkable for her beauty, cleverness, perseverance [and] generosity to her subordinates" and, in his battle report, the "best and bravest of the rebels"; one British officer felt that the "beast of Jhansi" had too easy a death. Her story became closely tied to Indian nationalism. Naming her in literature symbolised a nationalist spirit the British-controlled state media prohibited, and the independence movement saw her as a hero in the mould of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shivaji">Shivaji</a>. In 1943 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/subhas-chandra-bose">Subhas Chandra Bose</a> formed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rani-of-jhansi-regiment">Rani of Jhansi Regiment</a> of women volunteers, who served in support roles in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burma">Burma</a> and never fought in combat. Oral tradition linked her to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/durga">Durga</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lakshmi">Lakshmi</a> and Shakti, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indian-national-congress">Indian National Congress</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hindutva">Hindutva</a> proponents have each claimed her for their own aims.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dalit">Dalit</a> communities tend to view her negatively. Dalit writers have promoted heroes such as Jhalkaribai, whose accounts often present her as the Rani's equal or superior and sometimes call the Rani a coward, and festivals for the two women are attended by different communities. The best-known literary works are Subhadra Kumari Chauhan's 1930 poem "Jhansi Ki Rani" and Vrindavan Lal Verma's 1946 novel, which invented the quotation "I will never give up my Jhansi!" often attributed to her. No contemporary portrait of her survives.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>Lakshmibai (born Manikarnika Tambe between 1827 and 1835, died 1858) was queen of Jhansi, which the Company annexed in 1854 despite her appeals. She ruled it in 1857, her part in the killing of the British there remains contested, and she died fighting at Gwalior in 1858.</em></p>

